# signal-user

Herdr marks an agent that finished its turn, but a finish looks the same whether the agent needs the user, has a result for them, is waiting on a build, or is simply done. A signal says which, on its own line under the workspace name.

Signals are kept for workflows where they pay off, so the user can trust each one. Use this skill only when the user asks for a signal, or when the workflow you are running says to signal. The workflow says when; the rest of this page says how.

The signals:

- **✋🏽 ask**: stuck until the user answers. Look here first.
- **✓ ready**: finished something for the user to look at. Nothing is blocked; look when you can.
- **… wait**: waiting on something else that will wake the agent. Look away.

```bash
scripts/signal-user ask "<what you need, in a few words>"
scripts/signal-user ready "<what to look at>"
scripts/signal-user wait "<what you're waiting on>" -- <command> [args...]
scripts/signal-user wait "<what you're waiting on>"
scripts/signal-user clear
```

Each prints one JSON line. Outside a Herdr pane they print `{"status":"skipped",...}` and signal nothing (a wrapped command still runs), so there is no need to check first.

The script reaches Herdr through its local socket, `~/.config/herdr/herdr.sock`, and whether a sandbox lets it through depends on the harness:

- **Codex**: its sandbox blocks local sockets, so request escalated sandbox permissions on every `signal-user` command from the first call, rather than waiting for a refusal. In a wrapped `wait`, that permission also covers the command it runs.
- **Claude Code**: its sandbox already allows the Herdr socket, so run `signal-user` as an ordinary command.

## When it fails

A failure exits 1 with one JSON line whose `status` says what went wrong. Act on the status, not on a guess about panes:

- **`herdr_denied`**: a sandbox refused the Herdr socket. The failure says nothing about panes, because Herdr was never asked. If your harness can grant the permission, rerun the same signal command with it before looking into anything else. If it can't, tell the user that the sandbox blocked the Herdr socket.
- **`herdr_unavailable`**: Herdr could not answer for some other reason, such as a stopped server. `detail` holds Herdr's own message and `error_code` its code when it gave one. Tell the user rather than working around it.
- **`too_long`**: the reason is longer than `limit` characters (see [The reason](#the-reason)). Nothing was raised; rerun with a shorter one.
- **`unresolved`**: Herdr answered, but no single pane could be identified as yours. `reason` says why, and `candidates` lists the panes when several matched. Don't pick one yourself, because a signal under another agent's pane is one you cannot clear. Tell the user the signal did not go up.

A wrapped command does not run when the signal fails, so rerunning the whole `wait ... --` line runs it once.

## Picking the signal

Signal as the last thing before ending your turn, or before opening a question prompt, so nothing you do afterward reads as you having resumed.

- **✋🏽** when you cannot continue without the user: a decision between options, an answer, a credential or approval only they can give.
- **✓** when you finished and there is a specific thing for the user to examine: a PR you opened, a draft, a preview deploy, a report. Not for every finish; if there is nothing to look at, signal nothing. Herdr's own done marker covers that, and a ✓ on every finish would just repeat it.
- **…** when you are ending your turn while something you started will wake you: a background build or test run, a `watch-pr`, a deploy you are polling, another agent that will report back. If nothing will wake you, the wait isn't yours to signal; tell the user what to come back for and raise ✋🏽 instead.

Signal nothing for a finished task with nothing to decide, or a progress update. Herdr's own state covers a plain finish, and a signal that doesn't mean what it says teaches the user to ignore all of them.

## Waiting on a command

When the wait is a command, start it through `wait ... --` and run the whole line in the background, as you would the command alone:

```bash
scripts/signal-user wait "CI on #254" -- ~/.agents/skills/watch-pr/scripts/watch-pr --until ci 254
```

It raises the `…`, runs the command, and clears the `…` when the command exits, however it exits. The command's stdout and exit code pass through unchanged (the script's own JSON line goes to stderr), so you read the result exactly as you would without the wrapper. This is the form to prefer: the `…` lasts exactly as long as the wait, with nothing for you to remember.

When there is no command to wrap, such as another agent reporting back, raise `wait` on its own and run `clear` when the wait ends.

## The reason

The sidebar line has room for 30 columns at its widest, so a reason gets at most 27 characters after ✋🏽 (an emoji, two columns wide) and 28 after ✓ or `…`. The script refuses a longer one rather than let Herdr cut it off. Aim for a few words: `pick a migration strategy`, `approve the force-push`, `PR #254`, `CI on #254`, `staging deploy`. Leave it out only when it is self-evident from the workspace name.

## Clearing

The signals end differently. A ✋🏽 or ✓ ends when the user replies, so a Herdr plugin takes it down when your agent starts working again; don't clear it yourself. The user can also dismiss a ✋🏽 or ✓ from Herdr, for one they dealt with elsewhere. A `…` ends when the wait does, which can be several turns later, since the user may talk to you while CI runs; the plugin leaves it alone, and a wrapped command clears it on exit. Run `clear` for an unwrapped `…` when its wait ends, or to withdraw a ✋🏽 or ✓ you no longer need. Either kind comes down when your pane closes, and a 24-hour expiry backs everything up.

A workspace shows one signal, tied to the pane that raised it. `clear` leaves another agent's alone (`not_owner`). Your own newer signal always replaces your older one. Another agent's signal is replaced only by one at least as urgent, ✋🏽 over ✓ over `…`, so nothing that asks more of the user is hidden by something that asks less (`kept`). A wrapped command clears its `…` on exit only if that `…` is still showing, so it never takes down a signal raised after it.

For maintenance details and the reasoning behind ownership, lifetimes, and precedence, read [Signal design](references/design.md).
